// Copyright 2026 Jhon Magdalena Vila and the Sond contributors
//
// The byte-stream serial PORT (RFC-0014): a target-agnostic interface of three
// primitives, implemented per target by a `provides Serial for <target>`. A
// program depends on it with `use Serial`, and the compiler splices the provider
// that matches the build (a bit-banged UART on a microcontroller, a UART
// peripheral on other metal) and proves that provider against this contract.
//
// This is the INTERFACE only: it declares no bodies. The implementations live in
// the per-target libraries (e.g. lib/pic16/serial.snd), never here. Distinct from
// the file-descriptor serial of lib/x86_64/linux/serial.snd, which is a richer,
// hosted API over the OS; this port is the minimal byte interface a bare-metal
// node needs.

// Everything is a byte (`u8`): the smallest target this port must run on, an 8-bit
// microcontroller (PIC16F684), has no wider integer. So `serial_getc` BLOCKS until
// a byte arrives and returns it, rather than returning a wider "-1 for no byte":
// there is no room for a sentinel on 8 bits. A non-blocking read is a separate
// `serial_ready` predicate (added when a provider needs it), never a wider return.
//
// NOTE: today the compiler treats `u8` as a name for a machine integer and does not
// yet enforce its `0..255` range (that is RFC-0012, bounded integers). The interface
// already speaks in `u8`; when RFC-0012 lands the range is carried by the type.
pub port Serial {
    // Bring the line up (baud, framing, pin directions) before any byte moves.
    fn serial_init()

    // Receive one byte, blocking until one arrives. The value is a byte.
    fn serial_getc() -> u8

    // Transmit one byte.
    fn serial_putc(b: u8)
}
